  "query_preview": "(Today through Saturday)...\nIssued at 115 AM EST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nGuidance continues to depict a milder and somewhat more active \npattern, particularly during the middle of the coming week.\n\nA broad and disorganized upper level wave will continue to bring \nwidespread cloud cover to the area at least through tonight, and \nperhaps into Monday as well, though Monday will likely see more of \ntransition back to high cloud ahead of the larger and much more \nintense system set to impact the area mid week.\n\nPoPs will be kept to the slight chance or low chance categories \nthrough early Monday as moisture will be somewhat disjointed, with \nsubstantial low level dry air being gradually saturated from aloft, \nand the airmass aloft drying as this occurs. This may produce weak \nshowers or even drizzle as the lower level moisture shallows out \ntonight into early Monday, but in any case, any measurable amounts \nthat occur will be extremely light, with the primary impact of the \nsystem being the cloud cover limiting diurnal temperature ranges.\n\nFocus then shifts to the mid week, system, featuring a complex \nseries of upper level closed lows and a fairly potent surface \ncyclone - highly amplified upper level jet structure will lead to a \nslow moving system, which will likely provide a break of dry \nconditions at least through midday Tuesday if not longer. PoPs will \nbe highest with frontal passage Tuesday night into Wednesday, as \nstrongly boundary parallel flow is likely to confine much of the \nprecipitation to the immediate vicinity of the front.\n\nFairly strong shear will accompany the front, which may produce some \nembedded thunderstorms, and perhaps a low chance for a shear \ndominant strong storm or two in a high shear/low CAPE scenario, \nthough at the moment forecast profiles show poor lapse rates and \nvery minimal instability.\n\nLow shower chances may linger into the late week and perhaps \nportions of the weekend depending upon how the aforementioned \ncomplex upper level pattern plays out, which current guidance is \nvery inconsistent with. \n\nTemperatures will be mild but fairly solidly above normal early in \nthe week, with the cold front bringing them down, but still around \nseasonal normals, which for the Indy area are roughly 40 and 60 \ndegrees for lows and highs respectively.",
